r_pct":-15.211614,"inside_range":null,"source":"Fertilizers Europe BAT Booklet 5 (Urea), section 4.3, CO2 stripping, prilling, steam turbine drive","url":"https://www.fertilizerseurope.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/08/Booklet_5_final.pdf","note":"","kind":"energy","tolerance_pct":50,"pass":true}],"assumptions":["CO2 conversion per pass 60% at N/C 2.95 is the Booklet 5 design basis, not a computed equilibrium. H/C 0.45 and stripper efficiency are typical values.","Carbamate formation releases 117 kJ/mol and urea dehydration absorbs 15.5 kJ/mol (Wikipedia, Urea); NH3 and water vaporisation from the component data. Sensible heat in the loop is neglected.","Raw material losses are the mid points of the Booklet 5 emission ranges: prill tower dust 1.5 kg/t and NH3 0.85 kg/t, process water NH3 0.3 and urea 0.4 kg/t, vents NH3 0.3 kg/t.","CO2 compressor 1.1 to 145 bar, polytropic efficiency 0.78. Back pressure turbine 120 bar 510 C to 24 bar (IAPWS-97, 75% isentropic), exhaust heats the stripper; surplus LP steam drives a condensing section.","Evaporation removes 1.3 kmol water per kmol urea; process water heated 40 to 190 C with 75% interchange.","HP equipment in urea grade stainless steel. Prill tower costed as a 500 m3 vessel (rough)."],"notes":[]}